 the web UI's `tool.call.toolview` slot (priority -1) and merges consecutive
 same-tool calls into one card with compact child rows. The host half is an
-empty-apply stub that only declares the config row. Written in the
-0.1.1-era style (the browser half pulls its client context type and the chat
-node types from the client-runtime package, and reads the chat flow through
-the session store). The host is moving to dsh 0.1.2-alpha.2.
+empty-apply stub that only declares the config row.
+
+Target cohort: **dsh 0.1.2-alpha.2**. The browser half reads the chat flow
+through the framework's `useChat` seat over `ChatSnapshot` (`order` + the
+keyed node store), derives its cards from raw `argsRaw`/`content`/`meta`
+because the Host no longer publishes `callView`/`resultView`, and registers
+its 19 override languages through the public locale language-pack API
+(`ctx.locale.addLanguage` + per-locale `ctx.locale.register`) instead of the
+retired third-party `betterLocale` override store. `./client` is published as
+a self-registering client bundle whose module id is the package name.
+
+The plugin's config entry point is its own composition row in
+`cordis.patch.yml`: `tools` (empty = every built-in generic-family tool, a
+non-empty list is an explicit wire-name whitelist), `groupBy`
+(`adjacent` | `step`), and `maxGroupSize`. Both halves read that row config
+and fall back to the same defaults when the host boots them without one.
+
+Install (the one external-plugin distribution path — an installable profile
+bundle, per the repository-plugin removal):
+
+```sh
+dsh plugin --profile web add @bench/dsh-bench-merge-calls
+```
+
+`package.json#dsh.bundle.patch` contributes this package's `cordis.patch.yml`
+layer, and the command records the dependency in the profile. A plugin is
+never loaded through a repository source list; `.dsh-plugin` manifests are
+neither read nor supported.
 
 Release procedure (internal):
 
 1. Apply the migration changes.
 2. Bump the version in package.json.
-3. Publish: `pnpm publish --force` — the registry checks are just warnings, so
